{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits, ring‐closure operations (those that increase the number of rings) yield higher QED_delta improvements than ring‐opening operations (those that decrease ring count).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify ring count changes in successful edits and compare QED_delta between ring‐closure and ring‐opening subgroups.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter to murcko_scaffold_retained irrelevant and action_type any, but include only rows with non‐null input_smiles, output_smiles, and QED_delta. 2. Use RDKit to compute ring count for input_smiles and output_smiles for each row. 3. Derive ring_delta = output_ring_count – input_ring_count, then classify each edit as 'closure' if ring_delta>0 or 'opening' if ring_delta<0 (drop ring_delta=0). 4. Compute group‐wise summary statistics of QED_delta (count, mean, SD) and 95% bootstrap CIs (1,000 resamples) for closure vs opening. 5. Test both distributions for normality (e.g., Shapiro–Wilk) and select either Welch’s t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test to compare QED_delta. 6. Visualize side‐by‐side boxplots or violin plots of QED_delta for closure vs opening, annotating group means/medians and CIs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. DataFrame with columns [input_smiles, output_smiles, ring_delta, QED_delta, group_label]. 2. Table of count/mean/SD/95% CI for QED_delta by group. 3. Normality test results and chosen test statistic with p‐value. 4. Boxplot or violin plot comparing QED_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shorter natural‐language prompts lead to higher edit success rates (i.e., shorter instructions correspond to more successful edits).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Measure prompt complexity by token count and test its association with success probability across the full dataset.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; concatenate into one DataFrame and label each row 'success' or 'failure'. 2. Tokenize prompts by splitting on whitespace (or punctuation) and compute prompt_length = number of tokens for each row. 3. Define bins of prompt_length (e.g., quintiles or deciles). 4. For each bin, compute success_rate = (# successes)/(total rows). 5. Fit a logistic regression model (using statsmodels or sklearn) predicting outcome (1=success) from prompt_length as a continuous predictor. 6. Evaluate the regression coefficient and p‐value for prompt_length. 7. Plot success_rate vs. median prompt_length per bin with a trend line from the logistic model.",  
      "deliverables":"1. DataFrame with [prompt, prompt_length, outcome]. 2. Table of success rates by prompt_length bins. 3. Logistic regression coefficient, standard error, and p‐value for prompt_length. 4. Trend plot showing success_rate vs. prompt_length bins with fitted curve."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful water‐solubility edits (task_id=101), insert operations yield higher target_delta improvements than replace operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta between insert vs replace operations within task 101 for successful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to task_id==101, action_type in ['insert','replace'], and non‐null target_delta. 2. Group by action_type and compute count, mean, and SD of target_delta. 3. Bootstrap each group mean (1,000 resamples) to obtain 95% confidence intervals. 4. Test both groups for normality (e.g., Shapiro–Wilk) and select either Welch’s t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test to compare target_delta. 5. Create a bar chart of mean target_delta by action_type with error bars showing 95% CIs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary table: count, mean, SD, and 95% CIs per action_type. 2. Normality and group‐comparison test results (statistic and p‐value). 3. Bar chart with error bars comparing insert vs replace target_delta."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Within successful edits, those that break the Murcko scaffold have larger increases in molecular weight (MW_delta) than those that retain the scaffold.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the effect of Murcko scaffold retention on MW_delta in the successful edit set.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter to non‐null MW_delta and compute murcko_scaffold_retained boolean. 2. Group by scaffold retention (True vs False) and compute count, mean, SD of MW_delta. 3. Bootstrap mean MW_delta per group (1,000 resamples) to get 95% CIs. 4. Conduct normality tests on MW_delta distributions; choose appropriate comparison test (Welch’s t‐test or Mann–Whitney U). 5. Visualize MW_delta distributions for retained vs broken scaffolds using violin plots, annotating group means and CIs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of count/mean/SD/95% CI of MW_delta by scaffold retention. 2. Normality and group comparison test results. 3. Violin plot comparing MW_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The probability of an edit failing increases with the absolute magnitude of molecular weight change (|MW_delta|).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Model the relationship between |MW_delta| and failure probability across all edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; concatenate and label outcome. 2. Drop null MW_delta and compute abs_MW_delta = absolute value of MW_delta. 3. Divide abs_MW_delta into quantile‐based bins (e.g., deciles). 4. For each bin, calculate failure_rate = (# failures)/(total). 5. Fit a logistic regression predicting outcome (1=failure) from abs_MW_delta as a continuous variable; record coefficient and p‐value. 6. Plot failure_rate vs. median abs_MW_delta per bin and overlay the logistic regression curve.",  
      "deliverables":"1. DataFrame with [abs_MW_delta, outcome, bin]. 2. Table of failure rates per abs_MW_delta bin. 3. Logistic regression coefficient and p‐value. 4. Plot of failure_rate vs. abs_MW_delta with fitted logistic curve."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Successful task 103 edits (increase drug‐likeness) have smaller average MW_delta than successful task 105 edits (increase permeability).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare MW_delta between tasks 103 and 105 within successful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter to task_id in [103,105] and non‐null MW_delta. 2. Group by task_id and compute count, mean, SD of MW_delta. 3. Bootstrap group means (1,000 resamples) to obtain 95% CIs. 4. Test distributions for normality; choose Welch’s t‐test or Mann–Whitney U to compare MW_delta between task 103 and 105. 5. Create side‐by‐side bar chart of mean MW_delta by task_id with CIs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary table: count, mean, SD, 95% CI per task. 2. Normality and comparison test results. 3. Bar chart comparing MW_delta for tasks 103 vs 105."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful replace operations, fragments with higher polar atom fraction (N+O count / heavy atom count) yield larger QED_delta improvements.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Correlate fragment polarity ratio with QED_delta in successful replace edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter to action_type=='replace' and non‐null edit, QED_delta. 2. Extract replacement fragment via splitting on ' with '; parse with RDKit to count heavy_atoms and polar_atoms (atoms of type N or O) in the fragment. 3. Compute polar_ratio = polar_atoms / heavy_atoms for each replace. 4. Fit Spearman’s rank‐correlation between polar_ratio and QED_delta; record rho and p‐value. 5. Visualize with a scatter plot of polar_ratio vs QED_delta with a lowess or trend line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. DataFrame with [fragment_size, polar_atoms, polar_ratio, QED_delta]. 2. Spearman correlation coefficient and p‐value. 3. Scatter plot with trend line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits, adding rings (ring_delta>0) imposes a greater synthetic accessibility penalty (higher SA_delta) than edits that do not add rings.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare SA_delta between ring‐adding edits and non‐ring‐adding edits in the successful set.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter to non‐null SA_delta, input_smiles, output_smiles. 2. Compute ring counts for input and output molecules; derive ring_delta. 3. Classify edits into 'add_ring' if ring_delta>0, else 'no_add'. 4. Group by this classification and compute count, mean, SD of SA_delta and 95% bootstrap CIs. 5. Test for normality; perform appropriate comparison test (t‐test or Mann–Whitney U) on SA_delta. 6. Plot boxplots of SA_delta for 'add_ring' vs 'no_add', annotating means and CIs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. DataFrame with [ring_delta, group_label, SA_delta]. 2. Summary table of SA_delta by group. 3. Normality and group‐comparison test results. 4. Boxplot comparing SA_delta distributions."  
    }  
  }  
]}
